  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, you will determine what expressions would produce graphs with particular vertices. We will look more closely at how changing the \(h\) and the \(k\) in a quadratic expression of the form \(a(x−h)^2+k\) translates the graph, and recall that the coefficient \(a\) affects the direction of the opening of a parabola.</para>
<para>When you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
<list>
  <item> Describe how changing a number in the vertex form of a quadratic function affects its graph. </item>
</list>
<para>Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that will help you reach those goals:</para>
<list>
  <item> 7.17.1: Quadratic Expressions in Different Forms </item>
  <item> 7.17.2: Modify Expressions to Translate Graphs </item>
  <list>
    <item> 7.17.2: Self Check </item>
    <item> 7.17.2: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 7.17.3: Changing the Parameters of a Quadratic Expression </item>
  <list>
    <item> 7.17.3: Self Check </item>
    <item> 7.17.3: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 7.17.4: Transforming the Parent Function </item>
  <item> 7.17.5: Equations of Graph Translations </item>
</list>
<para>After that, you’ll <emphasis>practice and review.</emphasis></para>
<list>
  <item> 7.17.6: Practice </item>
  <item> 7.17.7: Lesson Summary </item>
</list></section>
</content>
